\section{Specify the Transfer at an Edge}

A register-transfer specification says what a register samples and when, including conditions under which it holds. Robert G. Plantz develops the positive-edge D flip-flop in chapter~7 of \emph{Introduction to Computer Organization}, then uses these building blocks for word-wide registers in chapter~8. An HDL normally describes the sampling behavior rather than drawing the gates of every flip-flop. Physical clocking still has setup, hold, distribution, and frequency constraints; the edge in an RTL simulator is an abstraction.

Consider a stage that receives a word and a valid bit on each rising edge. There is no backpressure. An invalid input inserts a bubble: the stage clears its output valid bit without overwriting the retained word. A reset must flush validity, but does not have to initialize the payload if downstream logic obeys the contract to ignore invalid data. That choice can avoid a reset on every payload bit. If invalid data is observable for some other reason, this policy is not necessarily acceptable.

\section{Reset the Contract, Not Necessarily Every Bit}

The example uses synthesizable SystemVerilog \cite{ieee1800systemverilog}. Synchronous reset has priority for validity. The enable on the data register deliberately disables writes during reset as well as during bubbles.

\begin{verbatim}
module pipe_stage (
    input  logic        clk, rst, in_valid,
    input  logic [15:0] in_data,
    output logic        out_valid,
    output logic [15:0] out_data
);
    always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
        if (rst) out_valid <= 1'b0;
        else     out_valid <= in_valid;

        if (!rst && in_valid) out_data <= in_data;
    end
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

\Cref{fig:visual-310} separates the two paths. No assignment to \texttt{out\_data} on an edge means hold, because this is a clock-edge process, not a combinational process. The state can map to a flip-flop with enable or to a flip-flop with a feedback mux. Before a reset edge, the simulator can show \texttt{x} for both outputs. After a reset edge it may still show \texttt{x} for the data, but the valid bit must be zero. The first accepted word replaces the entire payload.

