\section{Write a Transition Rule and a Reset Test}

This rule assumes binary control inputs meeting the clocking contract. The state on the right of an assignment is the old state; the final step commits the pair together.

\begin{verbatim}
at each rising edge:
    next_data = old_data              # hold through reset and bubbles
    next_valid = 0 if rst else in_valid
    if !rst and in_valid:
        next_data = in_data
    commit(next_valid, next_data)
# Test reset=1 and in_valid=1 together: reset wins.
# Release reset with in_valid=0: validity stays zero.
# Drive a valid payload: check both outputs after the edge.
\end{verbatim}

\section{Choose Synchronous or Asynchronous Reset Deliberately}

Asynchronous reset is a different interface contract. A SystemVerilog implementation of the valid flip-flop could use a process sensitive to both edges shown here:

\begin{verbatim}
always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) out_valid <= 1'b0;
    else        out_valid <= in_valid;
\end{verbatim}

The active-low reset assertion can then invalidate the output without a rising clock edge. Deasserting asynchronously near a capture edge can violate recovery or removal timing; use an appropriate reset release policy for each clock domain. A synchronous reset must instead be present at a sampling edge, and any asynchronous source feeding it needs appropriate synchronization. Neither spelling repairs a clock-domain crossing by itself. The Yosys \href{https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/latest/using_yosys/synthesis/proc.html}{process-conversion guide} documents asynchronous-reset recognition; recognition does not establish the target's reset timing.

\section{Scoreboard the Output After Commit}

Start the reference model with no known retained word. Drive the stimuli before each rising edge and compare only after the nonblocking assignments have committed. In the trace below, the first and last reset both clear validity. Only the last reset has a known previous payload to retain. The observable stream includes both accepted words, in order, but contains neither reset's input nor the bubble's arbitrary data.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccll}
\hline
Edge & Reset & In valid & In data & Output after edge \\
\hline
0 & 1 & 1 & 99 & Invalid, no known payload \\
1 & 0 & 1 & 17 & Valid, 17 \\
2 & 0 & 0 & 42 & Invalid, retains 17 \\
3 & 0 & 1 & 23 & Valid, 23 \\
4 & 1 & 1 & 51 & Invalid, retains 23 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
retained := none
received := []
for each (rst, valid, data) in stimulus:
    drive_before_edge(rst, valid, data)
    accepted := not rst and valid
    if accepted: retained := some(data)
    (v, d) := observe_after_register_commit()
    require case_equal(v, accepted)
    if accepted:
        require case_equal(d, data)
        append(received, d)
    else if retained exists and checking_this_implementation:
        require case_equal(d, retained.value)
require received == [17, 23]
\end{verbatim}

The last payload check tests this implementation's hold behavior, not the interface. Another implementation could write zero into the invalid data register and still satisfy the observable valid/data contract. Keep two versions of the check if the implementation is being replaced: a white-box check that diagnoses a lost enable on this RTL, and a black-box check that permits either representation. To test a two-stage pipeline, evaluate both next states from the same pre-edge snapshot. Passing the first stage's newly committed value into the second stage in the same call would remove a cycle of latency from the reference model.
